rmission for new women\u2019s and girls\u2019 football academy</p><p>A council\u2019s decision to allow Tottenham Hotspur Football Club to build a new training facility in a park in north London has been quashed by a judge at the high court.</p><p>The campaign group Guardians of Whitewebbs told a hearing in June that Enfield council\u2019s decision to <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2025/feb/12/dark-day-for-parks-plans-to-build-spurs-academy-on-london-green-space-approved\">grant planning permission</a> for a new women\u2019s and girls\u2019 footba","paywalled":false}],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"New Pension Schemes Act 2026 and Fit for Future regulatory framework came into force 30 June 2026, introducing enhanced governance requirements and mandatory new roles","lead":"Significant governance reforms require operational changes and new oversight structures. Committee turnover from elections may affect continuity of long-term investment decision-making.","iso":"2026-09-23","paper_title":"7. Appendix A - Draft Enfield Pension Fund Annual Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://enfield.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s115269/115269.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"External audit of 2025/26 pension fund accounts not yet completed at report date","lead":"External audit sign-off is essential for verifying the completeness and accuracy of pension fund accounts and identifying any control weaknesses or audit qualifications.","iso":"2026-09-23","paper_title":"7. Appendix A - Draft Enfield Pension Fund Annual Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://enfield.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s115269/115269.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Pension administration expenses increased 35% to \u00a32.1m, with cost per member rising to \u00a374.18 from \u00a359.66","lead":"Rising administration costs reduce the net return to the fund and increase the total cost of scheme operation. While partly temporary, they indicate higher baseline resource requirements going forward.","iso":"2026-09-23","paper_title":"7. Appendix A - Draft Enfield Pension Fund Annual Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://enfield.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s115269/115269.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Local Pension Board","paper_title":"7. Appendix A - Draft Enfield Pension Fund Annual Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://enfield.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s115269/115269.pdf","headline":"New Pension Schemes Act 2026 and Fit for Future regulatory framework came into force 30 June 2026, introducing enhanced governance requirements and mandatory new roles","detail":"The Pension Schemes Act 2026 received Royal Assent on 29 April 2026, and Final Fit for the Future regulations came into force 30 June 2026. These reforms introduce mandatory appointment of a Senior LGPS Officer and Independent Person, enhanced governance requirements, new training expectations, and strengthened roles for investment pools. Additionally, the fund's Pension Policy & Investment Committee composition changed following Enfield Council's May 2026 local elections. The fund is implementing a compliance programme to meet the new framework.","category":"governance","matters":"Significant governance reforms require operational changes and new oversight structures. Committee turnover from elections may affect continuity of long-term investment decision-making.","figures":"Fit for Future regulations effective 30 June 2026; PPIC membership changed May 2026 following local elections","quote":"Final Fit for the Future regulations came into force on 30 June 2026, introducing new governance and investment requirements for LGPS administering authorities.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Local Pension Board","paper_title":"7. Appendix A - Draft Enfield Pension Fund Annual Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://enfield.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s115269/115269.pdf","headline":"External audit of 2025/26 pension fund accounts not yet completed at report date","detail":"This annual report is presented in draft form with the external audit opinion outstanding. The report explicitly notes that the external audit opinion will be added upon completion of the audit. This is procedurally normal for interim reporting but the final signed accounts should not be relied upon until the audit is complete.","category":"audit","matters":"External audit sign-off is essential for verifying the completeness and accuracy of pension fund accounts and identifying any control weaknesses or audit qualifications.","figures":"Audit status: incomplete as of report date","quote":"The external audit opinion will be added upon completion of the audit of the 2025/26 accounts.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Local Pension Board","paper_title":"7. Appendix A - Draft Enfield Pension Fund Annual Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://enfield.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s115269/115269.pdf","headline":"Pension administration expenses increased 35% to \u00a32.1m, with cost per member rising to \u00a374.18 from \u00a359.66","detail":"Administration costs rose from \u00a31.56m to \u00a32.1m, driven by a 10% increase in payroll services charges and one-off costs for McCloud remedy implementation and pension administration system licensing. When one-off costs are excluded, cost per member falls to \u00a354, below the London average of \u00a358. However, the year-on-year increase represents a material cost pressure and signals potential ongoing resource requirements.","category":"overspend","matters":"Rising administration costs reduce the net return to the fund and increase the total cost of scheme operation. While partly temporary, they indicate higher baseline resource requirements going forward.","figures":"Administration expenses \u00a32.1m (2025/26) versus \u00a31.56m (2024/25), 35% increase; cost per member \u00a374.18 versus \u00a359.66; adjusted for one-offs: \u00a354 per member versus London average \u00a358","quote":"Administration expenses rose to \u00a32.1m, mainly due to a 10% increase in the charge for payroll services and one-off pension administration system costs.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Local Pension Board","paper_title":"7. Appendix A - Draft Enfield Pension Fund Annual Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://enfield.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s115269/115269.pdf","headline":"Investment returns below benchmark: fund returned 6.7% but underperformed benchmark by 5.0%, with significant weakness in equities (43% of fund, down 5.5%) and private equity (down 10.4%)","detail":"While the fund delivered positive returns of 6.7% in 2025/26 (improved from 2% in 2024/25), performance lagged benchmark across all time periods. Equities, representing 43% of the fund, underperformed by 5.5% over one year. Private equity significantly underperformed by 10.4% over one year and 12.2% over three years. Persistent underperformance directly erodes fund returns and could eventually require higher employer contributions.","category":"income","matters":"Sustained investment underperformance reduces the fund's ability to self-fund and could trigger contribution increases, offsetting or exceeding the benefit of the current 3% rate reduction.","figures":"Fund return 6.7% versus benchmark 11.7% (one year); underperformance 5.0% (1 year), 2.5% (3 year), 1.9% (5 year); equities underperformed 5.5%, private equity 10.4%","quote":"The Fund delivered positive absolute returns over all reported periods, returning 6.7% over one year, 5.8% over three years and 4.1% over five years. However, performance was below benchmark across each period, with relative underperformance of 5.0%, 2.5% and 1.9% respectively.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Local Pension Board","paper_title":"7. Appendix A - Draft Enfield Pension Fund Annual Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://enfield.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s115269/115269.pdf","headline":"Negative cashflow continuing but improving: fund paid out \u00a32.0m more in benefits than received in contributions, down from \u00a33.8m deficit in prior year","detail":"The fund experienced a negative cashflow position of \u00a32.0m for 2025/26, where benefit payments (\u00a374.7m) exceeded contribution income (\u00a372.7m). While this represents an improvement from the \u00a33.8m deficit in 2024/25, it shows the fund is dependent on asset sales and investment income to meet benefit payments. This is manageable but requires ongoing liquidity monitoring.","category":"other","matters":"Negative cashflow reduces the fund's flexibility, forces asset sales (incurring transaction costs), and increases exposure to market conditions. The improving trend is positive but the underlying maturity risk remains.","figures":"\u00a32.0m net outflow 2025/26 versus \u00a33.8m in 2024/25; benefits paid \u00a374.7m versus contributions received \u00a372.7m","quote":"For the 2025/26 year, the Fund remained in a negative cashflow position in relation to dealings with members, with payments exceeding income by \u00a32.0m. This is lower than the \u00a33.8m net outflow in 2024/25.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-15"},"name":"Enfield","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-22","since_iso":"2026-08-23","distress":false,"headline":"Fund administration costs increased 35% year-on-year to \u00a32.1m, driven by one-off system costs and payroll service inflation","lead":"Rising administration costs reduce the net returns available to the fund for investment. Structural cost increases are permanent and will persist even after one-off system costs settle. This creates ongoing pressure on fund efficiency.","also":["56 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Thriving Children and Young People Scrutiny Panel on 2026-09-22","n_findings":57,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"7. Appendix A - Draft Enfield Pension Fund Annual Report 2025-26","top_paper_url":"https://enfield.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s115161/115161.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":164,"news_drafts_total":3}}